# Manuscript PDF page 98

                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   “Mientras ella esté despierta,” said Abuela, quietly, “esa vaina no en‐
tra.”
   While she’s awake, that thing doesn’t get in.
   I stood there with the hot coin over the open face of a stone that had
been in my grandmother’s closet since before my mother was born, and
I thought: this is why she never left the house. This is why my mother
could. It was the first thing in my life that had made sense of the way
my grandmother stands in doorways.
   “¿Y su hijo?” I said. And her son? I didn’t know why I’d said it.
   My grandmother looked at me for a long moment.
   “Tiene uno,” she said finally. “Eso es después.”
   She has one. That’s for later.
   She began to wrap the stone, and as the cloth came up over the face,
the pressed place came creeping back into the merengue. A hair at a
time. Like a tide.
   “Abuela. Can I take pictures of these?”
   She looked at my phone, then at the pages, then at my phone again.
   “¿Para quién?”
   “A friend. Sofía. She reads documents for a living. Leases, permits,
the fine print. She can tell you what a landlord had for breakfast from
his signature.”
   “¿Es tu novia?”
   “No.”
   “¿Por qué no?”
   “Abuela.”
   “Lávate las manos,” she said, which settled it.
   I washed my hands. She put on her reading glasses, which she
didn’t need for this and wore anyway, to supervise. The rules arrived
one at a time, as I broke them. The pages stayed in their plastic: en el
plástico, sí; fuera del plástico, no. No flash. The 1789 page went on the
bedspread beside the 1961 newspaper, so the date showed in the corner
of the frame.
   “Para que sepan que no lo inventé yo,” she said.
   So they know I didn’t make it up.




